{
  "image_name": "5a7d98ed42081daf453fc9b4b897c10b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the canonical biogenesis pathway of microRNAs. It begins with miRNA genes transcribed by RNA polymerase II into primary transcripts that can be monocistronic, polycistronic, or intronic, all containing hairpin structures with the future mature miRNA sequence. In the nucleus, these pri-miRNAs are recognized by the Microprocessor complex, made of Drosha and DGCR8, which cleaves the stem–loop precursor. The drawing highlights the typical hairpin features used for processing, including a long loop and an approximately 35-base-pair stem.\n\nThis cleavage generates a pre-miRNA with a 5′ phosphate and a 3′ two-nucleotide overhang. The precursor is then exported from the nucleus by Exportin 5 together with Ran. In the cytoplasm, Dicer, assisted by TARBP and PACT, cuts the pre-miRNA to produce a short miRNA duplex, again with characteristic overhangs.\n\nThe final part shows loading of this duplex into AGO, aided by HSC70 and HSP90. One strand is retained as the guide strand in a closed AGO complex, while the opposite strand, labeled miRNA*, is discarded. Overall, the figure depicts the stepwise conversion of genomic miRNA transcripts into the active single-stranded miRNA effector.",
